   <conbody>
         <p>Admonitions are paragraph-level notices that separate advisory, safety-related, or supplementary information from the surrounding body content. Admonitions identify information that requires immediate reader attention — safety warnings, usage restrictions, helpful tips, important prerequisites, and cautionary notices — and give it structural separation from the prose that surrounds it.</p>
         <p>For readers and publishing systems, admonition apply stronger visual treatment or weight from tip through note through caution through warning through danger. Each one of them are distinct in admonition icons, background color, or text color.</p>
         <p>In well-structured HTML, admonitions are commonly authored using <codeph>&lt;div&gt;</codeph>, <codeph>&lt;aside&gt;</codeph>, or <codeph>&lt;p&gt;</codeph> elements with recognizable class names or labels that indicate the type of advisory information.</p>
         <p>The converter maps a paragraph with a recognized admonition style directly to <codeph>&lt;note type="[type]"&gt;</codeph>. For a bold inline label, it applies content pattern recognition: it reads the opening text, removes the label, and maps the remaining text to the matching note type.</p>
         <note type="important"> Use native HTML admonition structures, such as recognized class
         attributes or supported bold-label patterns. Do not simulate admonitions using bold text,
         colored paragraphs, or other visual formatting alone, as these patterns cannot be converted
         reliably into DITA <codeph>&lt;note&gt;</codeph> elements.</note>
